<a href="http://gangrey.com/1450">
<h2 class="design">In The End, Goodbye With Love And Dignity</h2></a>
<h4 class="byline">born to die</h4>

<p>

<a href="http://www.nytimes.com/2008/01/22/nyregion/22wake.html?_r=1&scp=4&sq=%22Andy+newman%22&st=nyt&oref=slogin">Andy Newman</a>: &#8220;It&#8217;s not a joke,&#8221; said the tall man in the F.D.N.Y. hat as he left the funeral home. &#8220;I mean, it&#8217;s somebody&#8217;s life, that just happened to pass away. Same thing&#8217;s going to happen to all of us.&#8221;<br />
<br />
The somebody was Virgilio Cintron, last seen, and forever to be remembered in the public imagination, being wheeled down Ninth Avenue in an office chair, dressed in a T-shirt and unbuttoned pants, as two of his friends tried to keep his corpse from sliding to the sidewalk.<br />
<br />
Monday was Mr. Cintron&#8217;s wake. Monday he was wearing a suit. And for the few dozen people in the world to whom Mr. Cintron was not a punch line or a future bar trivia question, it was a chance to say goodbye.<br />
<br />
A handwritten sign taped to the door of the R. G. Ortiz Funeral Home on West 72nd Street read, &#8220;This is a private viewing for family and close friends only!&#8221; <br />
<br />
The word &#8220;only&#8221; was underlined and was strictly enforced. Andrea Peyser of The New York Post got a quick glimpse of Mr. Cintron before she was ushered out. &#8220;He&#8217;s looking dead,&#8221; she reported. A reporter from The Associated Press was shooed away from the door like a dog.
