                            <a name="comment3356"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: In The End, Goodbye With Love And Dignity
</h4>

<p>
This is the sort of writing that takes an assignment that the reporter might have dreaded, and standing outside the funeral home with the other media probably felt a little dirty and sleazy jumping each mourner as they exited &#8216;what was he wearing?&#8217; <br />
But back in the newsroom, the whole thing is written in a way that takes the reader away from the tabloidness of the reporting. And as all political writers have learned, especially in this campaign season, if you write about other reporters covering something petty (candidate haircut, breakfast) it makes you seem separated from it all and a cut above.<br />
<br />
Here's how the NY Post handled the story:<br />
http://www.nypost.com/seven/01222008/news/columnists/bye_bye__bernie_207315.htm<br />
<br />
Classy.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:rabdulrahim@th-record.com">Raja</a>
	

 at February 01,2008 13:47
</div>
