                            <a name="comment3357"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: In The End, Goodbye With Love And Dignity
</h4>

<p>
Reading the NY Post piece, it's certainly no wonder why people don't trust us.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:dstockman@jg.net">Dan Stockman</a>
	

 at February 01,2008 16:20
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3358"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: In The End, Goodbye With Love And Dignity
</h4>

<p>
Raja brings up an issue I've thought about a lot, especially when I first started.  You end up in these situations where a person not working for a newspaper or TV station would back off - funerals, etc. -  because it seems the decent thing to do would be to let families have some privacy. But if you do, you miss an opportunity to do the reporting needed to make people real and human, like Andy did with this guy. I'd always think of Virgilio as the real life Bernie without that. Trouble is, how do you show people you're trying to do the important thing, not the Post or TV news thing, when you've got about a sentence to introduce yourself? Seems even tougher when there's a media pack standing nearby.<br />
I'd be curious to hear how other people deal with this.
</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:doyle.m.murphy@gmail.com">Doyle</a>
	

 at February 01,2008 16:25
</div>
<br><br>

                            <a name="comment3362"></a>
<h4 class="boldbyline">
Re: In The End, Goodbye With Love And Dignity
</h4>

<p>
thanks for the kind words people. i got the neighbor just by sticking around till everyone else went home. nobody wanted to be there, not just because of the assignment but because it was about 20 degrees and windy (this is cold in new york). but my bosses had big hopes for the story so i had to stay till i got something.<br />
<br />
and yes i know quite the well the trick about writing about other reporters writing as if i am above it all. it's a little cheap but i felt like i had to do something to be able to live with myself. in any case, i hope i didn't come across totally self-exculpatory.<br />
<br />
interestingly, i actually lost sleep later that night about having quoted andrea peyser, who was very nice to me and let me sit in her photographer's heated SUV, because she couldn't have anticipated she was talking to me for publication. what if she ends up doing a really sensitive story? i needn't have worried.<br />
<br />
as to the last question about how we scum pitch ourselves to the bereaved, it's pretty much a standard line  -- "there's been so much written about the guy and we just want to give a sense who he really is, and not just people talking sh4t about him." when you work for the ny times people are probably more likely to buy it than when you work for the post, of course, which may be unfair, but what am i supposed to do, not take advantage of the advantage?<br />
<br />
anyway, thanks again.<br />
<br />
andy<br />
<br />
<br />

</p>
<div class="posted">Posted by:


		<a href="mailto:anewman@nytimes.com">andy newman</a>
	

